**Corollary 6.4 (compactness, actual pointwise minima and vector frontier).**
$\mathcal K_s$ is nonempty and compact. For every positive five-mode test
input $\sigma$,

$$
\min_{\mathfrak F\ \mathrm{in\ Definition\ }2.3}
 p_H^{\rm acq}(\sigma;\mathfrak F)
 =\mathsf E_s(E^\dagger\sigma E),
\tag{CA.32}
$$

also with supported live high mass as objective. Each minimum has an
actual common attainer with at most twenty-four branches, dimensions at
most three, and genuine rank-three high matrices. The undominated
original twelve-vectors are exactly the undominated vectors of the compact
image

$$
\mathscr W_s=\left\{
 (\operatorname{tr}(\Theta(H_++H_-)\rho_{y,e,n}))_{y,e,n}:
 (H_+,H_-,L_+,L_-)\in\mathcal K_s\right\}.
\tag{CA.33}
$$

Each has such an actual finite representative.

Proof. The unit singular locus is compact, and its projector set is
compact. The six-atom theorem makes its convex hull the image of a compact
six-point simplex times six copies of that set. A bounded-trace slice of
$\mathcal C_2$ is the image of this compact hull times a bounded interval,
including zero. Thus the cone is closed. Each positive variable of
(CA.27) is bounded above by $I$, and all its other conditions are closed;
so $\mathcal K_s$ is compact. The common designs in §7, reused from JR,
prove nonemptiness for all $s$.

The objective in (CA.28) is continuous. Theorem 6.2 gives the lower bound
for every original design and Theorem 6.3 realizes a minimizing point.
Reassignment of a singular high atom cannot strictly decrease that chosen
positive objective, since it remains feasible. Hence it preserves the
minimum and yields the asserted genuine high representation. This proves
(CA.32), including zero, singular and subnormalized inputs. A complement
input has zero compression and minimum zero; a cross-support input uses
its unnormalized compression throughout.

Every coefficient vector in (CA.33) is achievable by Theorem 6.3, and
below every original vector Theorem 6.2 supplies a coefficient vector.
These two facts prove both directions of the undominated-vector
correspondence: any strict dominator in either set would give one in the
other. A nonzero singular high atom would weakly lower all twelve
coordinates on reassignment and strictly lower at least one, since

$$
\frac14\sum_{e,n}\operatorname{tr}(K(z)^\dagger K(z)\rho_{y,e,n})
 =\operatorname{tr}(K(z)^\dagger K(z)\operatorname{diag}(p_y))>0.
\tag{CA.34}
$$

Every $\operatorname{diag}(p_y)$ is positive definite. Thus an undominated
vector admits no such loss in its chosen spectral realization.

Compact reduction and actual attainment are credited to IR §11, whose
bound is thirty using nine low atoms per record. The additional cone
theorem replaces nine by six and gives (CA.31). It does not evaluate
(CA.28). The quantifier is $\forall\sigma\ \exists\mathfrak F_\sigma$,
with constants chosen from a public test state before execution and maps
common on all unknown inputs. It is not one design minimizing every
input. $\square$

The absence of a fixed JR-conditioned restriction is substantive. For
example IR §5's feasible baseline
$Z_+^0=\tfrac12\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&s&0\\s&1&0\\0&0&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)$
can, for $s<1$, be perturbed by a sufficiently small nonzero real multiple
of $Y_0=\operatorname{diag}(d^2,0,-h_0^2)$. Its effect image is
$\Theta(Y_0)=\operatorname{diag}(0,h_1^2d^2,h_2^2d^2-h_0^2c^2)$.
Both live coefficient matrices are initially positive definite, and
$M/2$ is strictly between $0,M$ on coordinates $1,2$. Small perturbations
therefore preserve $0\preceq Z\preceq I$ and
$0\preceq F_+^V-\Theta(Z)\preceq M$. Theorem 6.3 realizes them by
taking $L_r=0$. Thus interior binary live freedom remains in (CA.27).

